<p>This is a lookup table, not a ranking and not a buying guide. It records twelve major model releases whose own announcements were re-read on 21 November 2026. Each row keeps four fields that a citation actually needs: who released the model, the date on the lab’s page, the context window if that page states one, and the licence if that page names one. Prices and benchmark scores are left out on purpose. Both move, and a number copied from a launch post goes stale faster than the licence does.</p>
<p>A cell that says “Not stated” is not a zero and it is not a guess. It means the page linked from that row did not give the field when it was read. A second page is linked only when the date or the context window comes from somewhere other than the announcement, and that second page is named in the note. The wording in the licence column is the lab’s description. It is not this desk’s reading of whether the terms meet the <a href="/explainers/what-is-an-open-weight-model/">Open Source Initiative’s definition of open-source AI</a>, and it is not legal advice.</p>
<p>The rows sit next to the reporting that already quotes these posts. <a href="/topics/openai/">OpenAI</a>, <a href="/topics/anthropic/">Anthropic</a>, <a href="/topics/google-deepmind/">Google and DeepMind</a>, and the <a href="/topics/open-models/">open-weight hub</a> collect the stories. <a href="/explainers/what-is-a-large-language-model/">What is a large language model?</a> is the training term behind most of the product names. A world model, a speech model, or a weights file this desk has covered but did not re-open on 21 November is not in the table. Absence from the table is not a judgment that the release was small.</p>
<p>The same twelve rows are in a <a href="/data/ai-model-releases.csv">CSV download</a>. On a phone the table scrolls sideways. The short citation is under the table.</p>
