<h2>What the blanks show</h2>
<p>The useful comparison is not which model scored higher. It is which labs published the two fields a reader needs in order to cite the release, and which left them off the page.</p>
<p>Google’s Hydrogen post of 12 November and Neon post of 18 November each state a 1 million token window. Hydrogen also states an output cap of up to 1 million tokens. Carbon’s announcement of 28 October does not, and the later post that says Carbon is available does not add one. None of the three Google posts names a licence or says the weights can be downloaded.</p>
<p>OpenAI’s Terra, Cyber, and GPT-6.2 posts likewise do not state a context window or a licence name. Two of the dates are not on the product post at all. Terra’s system card says it was published on 26 October 2026. The GPT-6.2 system card says it was published on 17 November 2026. The Cyber announcement itself is dated 5 November 2026, and it limits first access to approved Daybreak Red customers. Those are access facts from OpenAI’s pages, not a finding about what the model can do.</p>
<p>Anthropic’s 9 November announcement says Claude Fable 5.2 and Claude Mythos 5.2 are the same underlying model, with Fable on general sale and Mythos behind verification programs. It names the API id <code>claude-fable-5-2</code>. It does not state a context window or a licence, and it does not say the weights are released.</p>
<p>The rows that do name a licence are the ones that also hand out a file. Mistral’s 29 October weights post calls the terms a modified MIT licence, the Mistral Ecosystem Open-Weight licence, and it adds one condition: a business that offers the model as a service needs a separate commercial licence. Moonshot’s model card says Kimi K3.1 Pro is under the Kimi K3.1 Pro License and lists a context length of 1,048,576 tokens. Runway’s 11 November post says Praxis-1’s weights and the code to run and fine-tune them are under Apache 2.0, including commercial use. The page does not give a text context window. It describes a 27-billion-parameter action model, which is a different specification.</p>
<p>Two rows are easy to misread from the headline alone. Tencent’s 4 November Hy4 post uses “open-source” in the headline and then says the weights and system cards are planned for the coming days. It does not name a licence and it does not give a token count. Alibaba’s Qwen4-Max-Preview page, last updated 16 November, lists a context length of 1 million tokens and does not name a licence. It dates the preview from 11 November through 25 November 2026 (UTC+8), and the same page says a production Qwen4-Max is available and recommended. This table does not turn that sentence into a second row, because the page does not give the production model its own date, context window, or licence.</p>
